# Transactions

## Framework semantics

Use connection transactions (`Connection::transactional()` / atomic save options) when multiple writes must commit or roll back together. Independent saves are correct when partial success is intentional.

## Required behavior

- Wrap multi-write consistency units (parent + children, paired ledger rows) in one transaction.
- Prefer after-commit side effects (mail, search index, webhooks) outside the DB transaction when failures should not undo the primary write.
- Do not use transactions as a substitute for application rules or validation.
- Keep transaction closures short and free of remote IO when possible.

## Anti-patterns

- Independent saves for an atomic business pair
- Bundling best-effort side effects into the same transaction forcing unnecessary rollbacks
- Long transactions that hold locks while calling external APIs

## Review checklist

- [ ] Atomic pairs use `transactional` (or equivalent atomic save)
- [ ] Non-critical side effects run after successful commit when appropriate
- [ ] Failure paths leave no half-written consistency unit
